From 3c8e4d10123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Antigravity <antigravity@gemini.local>
Date: Wed, 30 Jul 2026 12:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v4] drm/amd/pm: Disable deep sleep in PROFILE_PEAK

Deep sleep (GFXOFF/DS_*) power-gates the GFXCLK/UCLK/FCLK/SOCCLK/LCLK
domains between activity bursts to save power. The wake-up latency it
introduces is invisible to most workloads, but it is exactly what
AMD_DPM_FORCED_LEVEL_PROFILE_PEAK exists to avoid: PEAK is the level
callers force when they want the lowest possible response latency and
sustained peak clocks (e.g. VR compositors, pro-audio, latency
sensitive compute), and deep sleep undermines that intent by
reintroducing the wake latency PEAK is meant to eliminate.

Make deep sleep state a deterministic function of the forced level:
enabled for every level except PROFILE_PEAK. `level` is already this
function's own parameter, so this is tested directly once, right
after the switch that computes the clock ranges, rather than being
threaded through a new local variable set from individual case arms.
This also means switching away from PEAK to any other level restores
deep sleep unconditionally, instead of only on a path back through
AUTO.

smu_v14_0_2_set_power_profile_mode() already disables deep sleep
independently whenever PP_SMC_POWER_PROFILE_COMPUTE is the active
workload mode, and re-enables it otherwise, regardless of forced
level ("disable deep sleep if compute is enabled"). That call site is
driven by workload-mode changes, a separate axis from forced_level,
and both reach the SMU through the same adev->pm.mutex serialization
(see amdgpu_dpm_force_performance_level() and
amdgpu_dpm_set_power_profile_mode()), so reading
smu->power_profile_mode here is safe without extra locking. Since
profile mode and forced level can each change independently at any
time, this patch skips the deep-sleep toggle entirely while COMPUTE
is active, so it never clobbers that path's decision the next time
an unrelated level is (re)applied. For every other profile mode,
forced level is the sole authority.

The previous version of this change ignored the return value of
smu_v14_0_deep_sleep_control(). That's fixed here: a failed call now
aborts and returns like every other SMU call in this function,
instead of being silently ignored.

Signed-off-by: Antigravity <antigravity@gemini.local>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu14/smu_v14_0.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu14/smu_v14_0.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu14/smu_v14_0.c
@@ -1265,6 +1265,21 @@
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Deep sleep reintroduces wake-up latency that PROFILE_PEAK is
+	 * meant to eliminate, so keep it disabled while PEAK is forced.
+	 * COMPUTE workload mode independently disables deep sleep from
+	 * set_power_profile_mode() and takes precedence here: don't
+	 * re-enable it out from under that path just because a non-PEAK
+	 * level was (re)applied while COMPUTE is still active.
+	 */
+	if (smu->power_profile_mode != PP_SMC_POWER_PROFILE_COMPUTE) {
+		ret = smu_v14_0_deep_sleep_control(
+			smu, level != AMD_DPM_FORCED_LEVEL_PROFILE_PEAK);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
 	if (sclk_min && sclk_max) {
 		ret = smu_v14_0_set_soft_freq_limited_range(smu,
 							    SMU_GFXCLK,
-- 
